Paid files move behind the Worker, and a guard that keeps them there

Paul asked whether the files could be stored somewhere private until they are
paid for. Google Drive cannot do it: a share link is either public to anyone
holding it, or a per-person invite needing a Google account. Neither can ask
"did this person pay?"

R2 already can, and it is already deployed for the lesson audio.

- redeem_download(token) installed in Supabase. Security definer, granted to
  anon, because a guest has no session to check RLS against. It is the only
  way to turn an access_token into a product.
- r2-worker.js gains /download?t=<token>. It decides nothing itself; it asks
  Postgres and streams paid/<slug>.pdf from the private bucket on a yes.
- The plain media route now REFUSES any key under paid/. Without that line the
  bucket serves the paid file at a guessable URL on this same Worker and every
  other gate is decoration.
- Fails closed on a missing SUPABASE_URL/KEY, 503 rather than 404 on an outage,
  and one identical denial for every failure mode so a scanner learns nothing.
- private, no-store on the response. Copying the immutable cache-control from
  the audio route would put a paid file in a shared edge cache.

check-links.js now FAILS the build if any file with a price has a downloadable
file committed here. GitHub Pages publishes the repo root, so a paid PDF beside
its own sales page is already free to anyone reading the HTML. It keys off the
price in the registry, so a sheet that becomes paid later is covered the day it
changes. Positive control run: planted a PDF, it failed; removed it, it passed.

Nothing is leaking today. The $14 bundle has buy: null and no file at all, so
this lands before a paid file ever touches the repo rather than after.

NOT YET DEPLOYED. The Worker needs pasting into Cloudflare and two variables
adding, SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_KEY (the publishable key, not the secret).

